//! # Supervision
//!
//! A supervisor owns named child roles and fungible pools. It restarts failed
//! children under its strategy and restart budget.
//!
//! ```hew
//! actor Worker {
//!     let id: i64;
//!     receive fn work(value: i64) -> i64 { value + id }
//! }
//!
//! supervisor WorkerPool {
//!     strategy: one_for_one;
//!     intensity: 5 within 10s;
//!     child primary: Worker { id: 1 };
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let workers = spawn WorkerPool;
//!     match workers.primary.work(41) {
//!         .Ok(value) => println(value),
//!         .Err(error) => println(f"worker failed: {error}"),
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `one_for_one` restarts the failed child. The intensity clause limits a role
//! to five restarts in ten seconds; exhausting the budget leaves it unavailable.
//! Access a named child through its supervisor handle, as `workers.primary`
//! above. Pools add a `count` clause and expose indexed child access.
//! `one_for_all` restarts every child after one fails, while `rest_for_one`
//! restarts the failed child and children declared after it.
//! `simple_one_for_one` is for a homogeneous dynamic pool.
//!
//! Child restart policies are `permanent`, `transient`, and `temporary`.
//! Permanent children restart after any exit; transient children restart only
//! after a fault; temporary children do not restart. Nested supervisors apply
//! their own strategy first and escalate when their local restart budget is
//! exhausted.
//!
//! A child role resolves the current incarnation on a call. A direct incarnation
//! handle remains tied to that actor; restarting a role does not revive a stale
//! incarnation. Restart reconstructs declared state, so persist application data
//! elsewhere when it must survive a child fault.
//!
//! Calls on children wait for completion, including void handlers, and return
//! actor outcomes. A missing child or exhausted restart budget must be handled as
//! failure. Do not use a sleep as proof that a replacement is ready.
//!
//! `stop(supervisor)` requests graceful shutdown of children in reverse
//! declaration order; `terminate(supervisor)` requests cancellation.
//! `stopped(supervisor)` waits for the whole tree's terminal cleanup.
//!
//! Supervisor strategy and runtime support can differ by compilation target;
//! check the target's capability documentation before relying on supervision
//! outside the native runtime.
